# Solana
#
# Deny-by-default on JSON, because a Solana keypair is a bare JSON array of
# bytes and committing one publishes a private key. Kept deny-by-default
# rather than narrowed to id.json/*-keypair.json: a stray keypair under an
# unexpected name is unrecoverable, an over-broad ignore is merely annoying.
#
# The cost is that anything legitimately JSON must be allow-listed below —
# and that cost was already paid once. This rule is why no package.json
# existed anywhere in this repository: every frontend's manifest was silently
# never committed, which left four applications unbuildable from a clean
# checkout. Add to the allow-list when you add tracked JSON.
*.json
!tsconfig.json
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!.vscode/*.json
!**/assets/**/*.json
!mobile/ios/Runner.xcodeproj/**/*.json
# Asset catalogues. Every .xcassets folder is DEFINED by its Contents.json —
# the PNGs beside it are inert without one, so a clean clone had 19 tracked
# icon images and no catalogue telling Xcode what any of them were. This is
# the same failure the comment above describes, recurring in a new directory,
# and it went unnoticed for the same reason: the files exist on the machine
# that generated them. Matched on *.xcassets rather than the one path, so
# macOS or a second asset set cannot reintroduce it.
!**/*.xcassets/**/*.json
test-ledger/
.anchor/

